# Docs and the publish date travel with every GitHub push

Before `git push` of Meridian-lab code changes, update the docs and the published date in the same commit on `main`. The date is the calendar day the commit is pushed, in America/New_York. A later push on that same day keeps the date. A push on a new day moves every current published date together. Do not leave an older refresh date on the current release. Older version rows stay on the day they were published.

The AppImage build and the refresh workflow stamp that same America/New_York day. Do not stamp UTC.

Set that day in all of these:

- `CHANGELOG.md` — the current version’s “AppImage refreshed” date, the lab section date, a plain-language bullet for each user-visible change, and any packaging line that states when the current image was published.
- `resources/metainfo/io.github.dark1ltg.Meridian.metainfo.xml` — the current `<release>` `date`.
- `README.md`, `docs/README.md`, `POPULATE.md`, `docs/placement-pipeline.md`, and `docs/audio-analysis-pipeline.md` — the “as of” / “updated” date on each page.

Also update the page body when the behavior changed:

- `README.md` — when the short “what this is / how you use it” page would be wrong without the change.
- `docs/audio-analysis-pipeline.md` and `docs/placement-pipeline.md` — when listen or placement behavior changed.
- `docs/README.md` — when a guide’s promise changed.
- `POPULATE.md` — when setup facts changed.

Write the behavior the code has now. Push only after those updates are committed.
